The question asks what will be printed by the PHP code:
```php
$x = 5;
$f = function () use ($x) { return $x; };
$x = 7;
echo $f();
```

In PHP, when you use `use ($x)` (without `&`), it captures the variable by value at the time the closure is created. So `$x` is captured as 5. Changing `$x` to 7 after the closure creation does not affect the captured value inside the closure.

Therefore, `$f()` returns 5, and `echo` prints 5.

The statement says: "A private method of a parent is not part of the inheritance contract: a descendant can declare a method with the same name with any visibility."

Let me think about this in PHP:

In PHP, private methods are NOT inherited by child classes. They are not part of the "contract" that child classes must follow. A child class CAN declare a method with the same name as a parent's private method, and it can have any visibility (public, protected, private).

For example:
```php
class Parent {
    private function foo() {}
}

class Child extends Parent {
    public function foo() {} // This is perfectly valid
}
```

This works because private methods are not inherited. They exist only in the scope of the declaring class. The child class can define its own method with the same name and any visibility without conflict.

This is TRUE. Private methods are not part of the inheritance contract, and a child can override them (redefine them) with any visibility.

The statement is true - answer "yes".

The question asks what will be printed by the PHP code:

function inc(&$x) { $x++; }
$n = 5;
inc($n);
echo $n;

The key here is the `&` in the function parameter `&$x`, which makes it a pass-by-reference parameter. When `inc($n)` is called, `$x` becomes a reference to `$n`. Then `$x++` increments the value, which also changes `$n` from 5 to 6.

So `echo $n;` will print 6.

The question asks whether property hooks (get/set directly in property declarations) were introduced in PHP 8.4.

PHP 8.4 was released in November 2024, and it indeed introduced property hooks (also called asymmetric visibility and property hooks). Property hooks allow you to define get and set hooks directly on properties, like:

```php
class Foo {
    public string $bar {
        get => strtolower($this->bar);
        set => strtoupper($value);
    }
}
```

This is a PHP 8.4 feature. So the answer is True (yes).

The question asks whether PostgreSQL uses SAVEPOINT to rollback part of statements within a transaction.

This is true. In PostgreSQL, when you want to rollback a portion of a transaction (not the entire transaction), you use SAVEPOINTs. You can set savepoints within a transaction and rollback to a specific savepoint, effectively undoing only the statements executed after that savepoint was created.

Syntax:
- SAVEPOINT name;
- ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT name;

This is a core feature of PostgreSQL transactions.

The question asks whether in PHPUnit you cannot declare a dependency of one test on the result of another.

This is actually FALSE. PHPUnit does have a `@depends` annotation that allows you to declare dependencies between tests. For example:

```php
/**
 * @depends testSomething
 */
public function testSomethingElse()
{
}
```

This annotation makes a test dependent on another test's result. If the depended-upon test fails, the dependent test is skipped.

So the statement "In PHPUnit you cannot declare a dependency of one test on the result of another" is FALSE (the answer is "no").

